#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87ce1ae9-57eb-59cd-8da4-c1a0d3076afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453952} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff1072-dded-550a-b750-8d400dcf93f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453952} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e09413b-af06-58f6-88c6-125426ab31d4" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776c0c2e-2277-507d-b18d-ed1e84b4ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453954} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3dfc88-d38f-581f-9c20-80e64cbb04cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453955} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ee9fb1-8544-5a36-b32c-707df9287960" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ca50ed-9faf-5c4a-bf22-4d644e34ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453956} {:answer/id #uuid "161c0d73-cc78-5569-9199-8fc986254f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453956} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b63f72f-ca55-514b-bc37-6bef9f319ce0" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb1f3bd1-4155-5e86-902f-cd91483f7d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-74963" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a6788-72f7-50c1-a035-0612abea7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380244} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6b773-681c-58f9-b0ee-b6ee7442117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380245} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a1ac-d347-5b5f-9ae4-3572ba829da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380246} {:answer/id #uuid "91b1447d-4781-51f6-b677-110d8689bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} {:answer/id #uuid "8852c79a-f54e-5a8f-87c0-2803442d73d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f7b683-d099-548b-9b98-dddfeba7f07a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb25e06c-c7b0-5dbe-83ae-8f7b0c5dbf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-86868" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4da5c52-6b5c-54df-b17f-83bd497bbf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=x\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380652} {:answer/id #uuid "b86e90fc-a436-523b-9da9-b9d702be5d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-2\\,i+y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380653} {:answer/id #uuid "4def1406-b9da-59de-bc30-2b42c0b648cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380654} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd94f63-83b8-5cbf-89e4-6e021e2d4702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380655} {:answer/id #uuid "de1c9265-3201-5314-9c89-061c3e096082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380653} :answer-field/id #uuid "37acc21f-bf89-54d6-b6f6-f05fa5abc515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74301975-54ad-5b09-8f34-be154a2d8d2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122630" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ee68a4b95a815cc9372c4d6c1b739464de223375fa301d4cf3cdb1e6e5e829.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=x\\,i+j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-2\\,i+y\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c8f22e-9c72-5097-9d66-15b73bc21b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d2/d28dd5c26019ddf8dd9fa1c3034eed957cc5d62e433989d966f34c1623494966.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380714} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb393e8-f46f-59f9-bd05-a8b537c8b025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b896ff19664a92b34fd8456077b4a91ea2d36121c284001adfbf8216fc49a46c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380715} {:answer/id #uuid "b072cbaa-a0b3-5627-bae1-8d307f2b14e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b9/b96b3f9db529d096b96321e3efe9d645788c763373cfeeaeac559c343215e11a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380716} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6da738-e5c8-5567-b9f8-3a0e4086fc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b0/b0b3eced37ace6c64c65cf94c1621d6706894a079621870b19a69b0f27f2c842.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380717} {:answer/id #uuid "8558fc22-266f-5df8-9ed9-f72208859a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d1/d1fc9c2797e0fa16ba0c6b8f9328680c3706c118d5ed576d09be48e5068537fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380717} :answer-field/id #uuid "34658309-80b9-58e6-8164-617fe6aa19b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30d646c9-1021-587c-bc37-6244d8e4d4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122764" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{1-y}\\,i+\\frac{x}{y-1}\\,j\n$$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,3),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{1-y}\\,i+\\frac{x}{y-1}\\,j\\,:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,3) & =\\frac{2(3)}{1-3}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{3-1}\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{1-(-1)}\\,i+\\frac{2}{(-1)-1}\\,j=-i-j \\\\ F(4,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{1-2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{2-1}\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/b0/b0b3eced37ace6c64c65cf94c1621d6706894a079621870b19a69b0f27f2c842.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85813fa1-7598-522f-8c21-a78340a56373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380959} {:answer/id #uuid "794f355d-1fd3-5f85-acbc-2a0b9cb24276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380960} {:answer/id #uuid "d727996c-f099-52f9-bb09-3a563b0e8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380961} {:answer/id #uuid "169f002f-fd26-50ae-930c-9a604e9ae96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380962} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d17484-2506-5d97-98a5-73a7fa14daee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380963} :answer-field/id #uuid "391d0c46-ac45-548f-b507-5755302aa0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9414edde-aaa5-55fe-99cf-fcbd12cf83ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-132255" :question/problem "For the function $g(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂g}{∂z}$ at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the constant multiple and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(y{z}^{2})}^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\right)\\cdot y\\cdot 2z \\\\  & =-\\frac{2yz\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}{z}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(3,\\,1/2,\\,1)} & =-\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(1)\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}(1)^{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c844163-eb89-5874-92d7-a5e42c8fbbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+y)}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381246} {:answer/id #uuid "30d11849-9588-53e0-81ab-c140edee486b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381247} {:answer/id #uuid "50a71ffc-fa50-51cd-9ad1-844d4f0ba6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381248} {:answer/id #uuid "8816e80b-49b5-5210-ae66-28bae9df4a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381249} {:answer/id #uuid "f228610a-936e-57b2-b0d3-3a75eb518312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x+y)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381247} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0221042-a90c-56d3-89c0-3048bc883643" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4560fcbf-c2fb-5e9f-b888-53c870445e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-159655" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot 0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc2a41c-8b46-55d8-8633-185639106579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381253} {:answer/id #uuid "b53ff0ab-76f0-50e1-b576-7d4bec199596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}y}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{3}y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381254} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7858-5a4c-5e37-baac-5e1b09598456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}y}{x+{x}^{3}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381255} {:answer/id #uuid "75c5ac00-44da-5d0f-bc6a-05231039cb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{x+{x}^{3}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381256} {:answer/id #uuid "891569ad-a562-5cb2-8249-28d2b2b8e614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}y}{1+{x}^{3}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381255} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d337f9-2d19-54ee-833d-730d6117510a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6cc4635c-e2d0-54e1-a8b8-18c5b785e7cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159659" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{3}y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{3}y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{x}^{3}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{x}^{3}y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\cdot (1+y\\cdot 3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}y}{x+{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "764fe799-f9d4-56fc-8786-768351e90b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381260} {:answer/id #uuid "af40a1cc-fcdd-5d82-88f1-afb1cb810dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381261} {:answer/id #uuid "238d2bb5-2057-5938-92cb-490c8fd1d052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381262} {:answer/id #uuid "007df772-538e-5628-b388-eb1493c3de4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381263} {:answer/id #uuid "e31f346f-c25e-5e5e-9897-5f3acda1e293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381261} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cf4f0d-d3ae-5f4a-af95-43db53f9c0fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba29a24e-25aa-5034-8849-e753260af75f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159706" :question/problem "For the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},\n$$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,-\\pi )} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1)(-1)}{(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8367eed8-8db9-5b16-8030-4fb1d45343f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381267} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f7d926-4b2e-5a47-a964-89bbf3409d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381268} {:answer/id #uuid "c0684d01-ce2e-5d72-b7c5-5083711bc071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381269} {:answer/id #uuid "3e29fb6f-8142-5709-a889-a0d7208a0ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381270} {:answer/id #uuid "20723cc1-5581-5f10-b006-0cc5888b60c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381267} :answer-field/id #uuid "c43d33af-7b21-5589-9d8f-5fccd66d3600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f6e1b67-30d1-5d26-980f-cea342aa0dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159707" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}\\right) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y-x})}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot (-1)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{y-x}}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,-1)} & =\\frac{2(1)+{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+1-1}{{e}^{-2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b4a1d9-cd1c-5571-9518-79d29836ff46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381274} {:answer/id #uuid "12f38844-8972-566b-9cc4-9cfde5403c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381275} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c9d0a7-3b5f-5c87-8d2d-3652e6d79b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381276} {:answer/id #uuid "83292db4-d816-5e9e-a3de-33ff1b3276fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381277} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8d819d-5775-5389-a036-8dc2fb90c6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381277} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc5a44e-5e88-5654-addf-13837aea580f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9fb48fb-ee18-53c2-9d40-be28dc7c4677" :question/math-academy-id "q-159722" :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.\n$$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5361b3e-3a8f-5f23-bccc-16c3847e243d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+{y}^{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381281} {:answer/id #uuid "c396d8bd-ee03-54b5-9fa3-772cead8f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+{y}^{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381282} {:answer/id #uuid "466b5ac2-73bd-559a-876d-a603caed3fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381283} {:answer/id #uuid "807ec85c-b29c-57a4-8b0d-6e20c421ffd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+{y}^{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381284} {:answer/id #uuid "89d9d8d2-74da-542b-b631-e8a3e5161e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+5{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381281} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1af9e5e-7d6f-5413-99e4-88f6a6ae2bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3444068d-2f79-5306-813a-47bbdab330c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159725" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{y}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{y}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+{y}^{5}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(1+{y}^{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c1021c-0885-5bf7-b3e5-6327c277df4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381288} {:answer/id #uuid "110927d7-7b2d-5c08-bf3d-0346fca0acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381289} {:answer/id #uuid "60bf5676-3040-5ff2-acf5-f790c6a36101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi +1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381290} {:answer/id #uuid "92b9f3d2-b458-5615-b4f0-1138a3573da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381291} {:answer/id #uuid "d23a20cb-a2b4-58ef-a4f6-d0f3bb9feba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381289} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a23d4f6-a890-5ca9-9d9c-0ca9c46113c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39e99179-93fc-5477-adbb-fdfcbcd0de54" :question/math-academy-id "q-159753" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}),$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) & =(2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi /4,1)} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2})+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd3a4ba0-0931-5b38-80b6-0a4eb0ceef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381295} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcd4e39-e404-5742-b04b-6b4daf7838dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381296} {:answer/id #uuid "e72dc8ba-5b49-5a51-819e-098dd8a6604c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381297} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cf94f7-cef2-596a-922d-a751bbffe777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381298} {:answer/id #uuid "f4796136-2991-5ef1-956c-7078ef642165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381299}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381295} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e96d639-7583-5d45-a531-55e609338b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "079e858b-c02c-58b3-bb61-a7e782e9db9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159774" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y}+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x((2y)\\cdot {e}^{y}+{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}) \\\\  & =(2y+{y}^{2}){e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,2)} & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2}){e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7c02e33-edf5-5e9a-9dd7-81cb55213e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381919} {:answer/id #uuid "2603ddd8-ff44-5138-990c-fd4a215965d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381920} {:answer/id #uuid "48cc0f63-6ffb-5e32-995f-dc1c121b3cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381921} {:answer/id #uuid "177336c7-0dc2-579c-a40e-ed3d123fed1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381922} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd8816e-4b58-5f53-b267-eff8ba589cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381919} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585b134-bc39-5ad2-9671-a9693974496c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1970f3e5-319e-5450-8a91-5ac540e3ae28" :question/math-academy-id "q-35715" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the constant multiple and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot (x) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37ea7ae-4cef-5fcd-9228-e2df831da5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382126} {:answer/id #uuid "679825cc-7f11-5343-ae88-84a1ca97e167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382127} {:answer/id #uuid "0df78ffc-b050-5411-91a2-dfd4d4406f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382128} {:answer/id #uuid "3cea194b-2a28-5191-a2e3-0a7f014011fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382129} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f3664-511a-5234-bd52-b3ad5619722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382127} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d9e209-65c0-591c-9c57-813c74680ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "684028b0-fca2-5e81-bf94-98e01d24397f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64906" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(-1,1)} & =3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+(1){e}^{(-1)(1)} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faffc889-7ad7-5317-8313-d46fd721c933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{z}^{4}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382147} {:answer/id #uuid "f262f116-4129-511f-91c6-6ef599a2213c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382148} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1efe75-1502-501f-aabc-57c0b0f621d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}y+4{z}^{3}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382149} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad3668-cb67-53a3-8444-7e9d5683bcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382150} {:answer/id #uuid "7298654a-25b1-5bdf-8284-a89f479aed3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}y-{z}^{4}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382150} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ca4d90-e0e6-5e67-8e34-f7e4581124f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7f2e5426-fec3-56e2-a783-8f63ba33ab35" :question/math-academy-id "q-70022" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}) & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac4949ac-c2c5-5400-9b81-caf9da897244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382154} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb86e61-8965-50a0-9c65-7710e3f23e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382155} {:answer/id #uuid "ca85cdde-ed68-5dfd-a8df-8739efb9ad70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382156} {:answer/id #uuid "360dc3a3-e4a5-57c2-9e1c-028e7af0458b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382157} {:answer/id #uuid "493e03c2-21cb-58c1-95b8-a3d64817cab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382154} :answer-field/id #uuid "524bf164-94aa-5c0e-a84e-da7f0acbfdd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e55c37e1-12b0-54db-abbc-4811c1a7e4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70030" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1,2)} & ={2}^{3}-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\  & =8-2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =8+2 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83468821-c39c-5688-9232-e0a48bb155ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382980} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b76065-e59a-5ca7-9bfb-b669207ba5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x+4y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2x+4y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382981} {:answer/id #uuid "e411cee5-3a6c-5dda-a344-184fd78d28cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2x+4y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{2x+4y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382982} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cc5482-1799-5098-a009-2ed159b19110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x+y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382983} {:answer/id #uuid "62c2233e-e9a0-53da-b0de-07b7e76c7fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b277d7-41a9-51d5-9221-157f88442b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdc51975-a7a3-5414-a1fb-3bb1994c28af" :question/math-academy-id "q-122599" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc65a4b-da4d-5350-953b-f73c08bd26cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(z+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+(x+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382987} {:answer/id #uuid "36b97e42-68ff-502f-862c-54abe712f096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382988} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f60994-da11-59b0-bb31-edf3cb7c1617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382989} {:answer/id #uuid "344a1d8c-370f-5d8f-9dd1-49c0513501e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382990} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2fc14b-bb86-5f37-bc2d-be43a95e0d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382988} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3294c69-216b-5d6f-aa53-6f52d4c57c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cde4da3d-ec55-5f32-ac16-c33bd9acfbfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122600" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1393202f-d724-535d-8136-babf2686d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{xyz+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{xyz+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{xyz+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382994} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f87687-52a9-5e53-b8e0-28200e4a81f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382995} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb0e8f2-8c32-5a43-9f8c-3b04ae890241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382996} {:answer/id #uuid "b14f2ad2-72a2-5e49-bfaf-d442c2b9f197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382997} {:answer/id #uuid "026ff4bf-c0ad-56d4-bab2-b4e07f1395b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382997} :answer-field/id #uuid "640d3877-8db5-5d72-a910-f0495ca7df56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c11364f-7b1f-5610-8765-613e6f80f65c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122601" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605c17ba-53c0-5224-8301-c7161947f7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(6x{y}^{2}+{z}^{4})\\,j+(x-4y{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383001} {:answer/id #uuid "100cf2d7-b1cf-56bd-b4c5-b50e52dddf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383002} {:answer/id #uuid "cb18d096-f5eb-5578-ad2c-c68d07d3455d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383003} {:answer/id #uuid "8aee926a-f13b-525d-8f4f-67de6743fd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{2})\\,i+(6xy-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-8{y}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383004} {:answer/id #uuid "5fca2b8f-91e9-5173-9378-858ea3cdc4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+{y}^{2})\\,i+(3xy-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-8y{z}^{2})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383003} :answer-field/id #uuid "732edbd7-fdd5-57cd-93ab-a2bf9f45302a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9bc952c-6483-5cda-bd93-71113b0eb352" :question/math-academy-id "q-122617" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2xz+{y}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8bec6eb-8e0a-5ce5-966f-3cfc2e175f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383008} {:answer/id #uuid "eee84ab8-4f31-5f75-abfa-e9d0d3f0fc7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d9/d91a13ce4ee54923d33ab5df7506ce8200d02f53695d85d186e0c9f5dc9345db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383009} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0f9738-4804-5496-8f1c-2a371d83d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/57/57ab7ae667788fe307f67df7078d91ee8c0e82f9703d1c59c439a790af9b2fbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383010} {:answer/id #uuid "837b5ab1-a135-59d4-bd04-ec207c823e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/22/2215b79d50f88933aa2b83eb76251eb97a9f3491ffc86b0c5e0eba3debd80cdd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383011} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be86a5-a237-5718-8d27-339f9839dbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/94/94f8ffc9b7e98188d2a0429ada7f936df7816e9a04d42e97d3b57e6ab34cfe41.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383008} :answer-field/id #uuid "f519e720-525e-5a35-9830-b680df8d1973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b63c956-bea1-5b1b-a3bb-61d243984ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122687" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =(-2)\\,i-j=-2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i-j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i-(-1)\\,j=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaab34-479d-5252-a2e1-4b9b7d0982eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/73/736cd2cf5d297363c4546465ef46476107cab0e5ec12de51d1792df827dc6c16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383015} {:answer/id #uuid "096acd8c-d44b-588e-a2a6-4c831113ad73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1b/1b7d5a942e7a4df8f0b1e71051fa527213d2e43abaf411bc5024c66f90208e92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383016} {:answer/id #uuid "761f05d0-5903-5c2f-900e-e1fb72b55741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8efbe2acdfe8530f68a51bf4217c2d7689c491f3a8a412e6f3777cb12932e0fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383017} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0d60eb-ac2d-5679-8076-b7429123e536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/235d03d87913ebe00b51a0643920c9565e8ffcb88ab32c63bf47ce3b39429de0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383018} {:answer/id #uuid "98acb1ee-bc76-53a3-80b8-02da68d6b4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383019} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec7584e-69da-56ab-a5cb-c7e9ee2cc549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "412a3f65-3326-5eb4-8063-61b8d30c5ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122708" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={2}^{-2}\\,i+2\\cdot (1)\\,j=\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & ={2}^{0}\\,i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & ={2}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6926dfa-4ca0-5d9b-bb91-d663dc9fd39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9b/9b8e151985327da40d1fb3044c7f4c785a9f0fcbaa5d71d7f865d4b3fee38ab9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383022} {:answer/id #uuid "2226c493-e827-540e-b916-5dff3335baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383023} {:answer/id #uuid "1faef452-1968-5f42-b62d-394dc63eb8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} {:answer/id #uuid "77afea4d-6f7d-5b97-a1b1-fcdcc7652a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/04/04f52ccb3eed1f0377edebf34607812f2c8eefe27e910e250959e8fa39ef13d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383025} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f42144-a862-5e50-bcf2-5c21c8fe56a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ae/ae9e6dc29361d66784dae2868e500940132049cfa04b65aafddc06d35c1afdbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} :answer-field/id #uuid "800aaf33-50f4-54f1-a1a6-6a6d09e8c947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8afa4c36-ef37-5de7-ade0-942dd686e265" :question/math-academy-id "q-122790" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ef1b203d6d88d8af1517b91b02df2f652b4e68fe13a28473924c067273937b2.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/3c/3c2cbbc268e070fa6a9ad887d06d94e73db19a70337bff2f9f11d67973d02898.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ac919f-1bd9-5b74-83e5-31b1fb7eb831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/93/9355e5a989137d0d74ee803bf0fb0248fc68e4df4aa1843afede93d1bb506a45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383029} {:answer/id #uuid "da18eb00-5c0a-593a-8196-d37fb9930291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bc/bcdc3fe01d59e4f2e37b702925056712cc8ae8d0f4d0b7debf002bbc4c27654c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383030} {:answer/id #uuid "21421b38-5138-523b-9081-5643dff4c2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/46/4663eaaf8c31781ebffe486d2f5a4e4036173114d13cf1a77b8a8f8d67603a29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383031} {:answer/id #uuid "221f995d-6011-5cf9-9cb1-b16eb83bc1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/23fa66fe343603112a28c4c97db76a4a58c56468b756654af0c426ea031e1065.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383032} {:answer/id #uuid "03dafc83-b8c9-596e-ade6-d66b6c98a145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dcd7de7b52c59ceda71245ef18d7cb1327a0e3f3b93f6f8e0bb8c5e386958217.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383033} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82091c2-cd87-50e1-b7f0-6a0dfeafcc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c721d422-27d6-53ce-b590-0c95850da8e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122802" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b58ade40a7e290cba486dfa43717d034036473b1fa5e71ab36fe281e65bc8958.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/d2/d23ebc22ef66bd6af9c9347f64113f78665eae84e44b235c4cd968f6885e14f5.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/dc/dcd7de7b52c59ceda71245ef18d7cb1327a0e3f3b93f6f8e0bb8c5e386958217.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db9d9fc-22a0-597f-aae9-81bb345368b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383085} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecab5b2-5c74-57ec-9424-e1622a043b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383086} {:answer/id #uuid "59d1274a-8060-550f-9c16-15badbd51936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383087} {:answer/id #uuid "89408273-38c8-5f7c-aaa3-d98a11b3ce1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383088} {:answer/id #uuid "e06e7ee3-6e0b-56c9-a951-2e2c02187b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383088} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0e2a52-799a-5e23-b506-14c0bcd60b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0611e4b-e342-5f93-a418-5af6bc5c2cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-127650" :question/problem "If $h(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right),$ find $∇h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "514f6510-65a6-5c71-8eca-34568a35dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1})j+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383092} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1164dc-4f74-5f63-8956-517c46e081b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+z{y}^{z-1}\\,j+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383093} {:answer/id #uuid "37e9d54a-963a-50c9-af62-5869773b3bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j+({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1})k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383094} {:answer/id #uuid "daceaefe-76b2-57db-9142-cb9036ed18b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1})i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383095} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a91458-30b5-5353-b216-4f46dc771583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+z{y}^{z-1}\\,j+z{y}^{z-1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383092} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7309b8-5993-5bb2-9ecd-ce38b1c0b74d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf052c6a-faee-5b8c-b178-4c397426507c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127945" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{y}+{y}^{z},$ find $∇f(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1})j+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8c5a90-83e7-5f9f-b520-1eaae67e52b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e(i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383099} {:answer/id #uuid "de50d21e-e598-5ea9-bc0f-adeed68ca073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e(i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383100} {:answer/id #uuid "86a0af6e-4435-566d-a878-c8e589b685c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}(-i+\\pi \\,j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383101} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1d0ab5-cfea-5a4b-924a-7f3b508a6a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{e}(i+\\pi \\,j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383102} {:answer/id #uuid "ce237491-8288-54ff-835f-4ec66f6ccaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{e}(i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383103} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2fd85f-4bd0-5b36-b61c-e043947f1967" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "804d417e-4fe8-53d3-ae3f-56da60f7d4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127947" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,\\pi )$ if $h(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)+{e}^{xy}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))(z) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,j+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\,i+{e}^{xy}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j-y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\,i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)\\,k) \\\\ ∇h(\\frac{1}{2},1,\\pi ) & ={e}^{(1/2)(1)}(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\pi )\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\pi ))\\,j-1\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\pi )\\,k) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}(-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-0\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{e}(i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "763b5a77-6493-5261-b8d3-54d89d3556f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\,i-({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383106} {:answer/id #uuid "35f70d4c-b368-5785-8f2b-55de44f7f968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}((2x-3y)\\,i-3x\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383107} {:answer/id #uuid "02ee0e10-1bfe-5fcd-b946-23648b7beb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+3xy+3x)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383108} {:answer/id #uuid "00dc6b24-907b-54d8-b5de-b9d4002d1f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy-2x-3y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+3xy+3x)\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383109} {:answer/id #uuid "b3105e5f-7b64-505a-a465-56ea29d0bb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy)\\,i-3x\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383106} :answer-field/id #uuid "864fbb12-509a-5c57-bdf6-96d7c651bd26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb82a957-3ae8-5e58-8924-0d152fcf4c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-128532" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{x-y},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-3xy,\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i-3x\\,j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h∇g+g∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3xy)({e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j)+{e}^{x-y}((2x-3y)\\,i-3x\\,j) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\,i-({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\,j)+((2x-3y){e}^{x-y}\\,i-3x{e}^{x-y}\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\,i-{e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\,i-({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1239f96f-2e4e-508d-a9f2-75796efa86a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}(4\\,i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383155} {:answer/id #uuid "45a77767-f45c-5c88-af82-5221a5fbd33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383156} {:answer/id #uuid "33650346-32bd-534a-a64c-45e97e73fd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,j-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383157} {:answer/id #uuid "64e6389b-f8ce-50bf-a097-05a8b301d96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383158} {:answer/id #uuid "d73cf648-7973-5ea5-aece-b31b3cb3d9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}(2\\,i+4\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383156} :answer-field/id #uuid "876e06ec-0ab3-5cfb-b188-15f5d0a5be15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cee7213b-050a-5cea-8e38-6fcbc59025d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129892" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(-1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-2)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-2,-1)}{‖∇f(-2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2\\,i-4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
